WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:05.036 --> 00:07.666 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:16.336 --> 00:22.326 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:23.426 --> 00:27.816 align:middle
As you know, the Federal Open
Market Committee this afternoon

00:28.416 --> 00:33.326 align:middle
reaffirmed the current 0 to one
quarter percent target range

00:33.326 --> 00:35.126 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

00:36.026 --> 00:38.586 align:middle
We also updated our
forward guidance,

00:38.646 --> 00:41.696 align:middle
indicating that an
increase in the target range

00:41.696 --> 00:45.346 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate remains unlikely

00:45.346 --> 00:47.146 align:middle
at our next meeting in April.

00:48.456 --> 00:52.246 align:middle
With continued improvement in
economic conditions, however,

00:52.776 --> 00:57.226 align:middle
we do not want to rule out the
possibility that an increase

00:57.226 --> 00:59.736 align:middle
in the target range
could be warranted

00:59.736 --> 01:01.286 align:middle
at subsequent meetings.

01:02.296 --> 01:05.586 align:middle
Let me emphasize,
however, that the timing

01:05.586 --> 01:09.676 align:middle
of the initial increase in
the target range will depend

01:09.676 --> 01:13.756 align:middle
on the Committee's assessment
of incoming information.

01:15.156 --> 01:16.526 align:middle
Today's modification

01:16.526 --> 01:20.146 align:middle
of our guidance should
not be interpreted to mean

01:20.486 --> 01:23.856 align:middle
that we have decided on the
timing of that increase.

01:24.976 --> 01:29.796 align:middle
In other words, just because
we removed the word "patient"

01:30.596 --> 01:35.196 align:middle
from the statement doesn't mean
we are going to be impatient.

01:36.826 --> 01:39.706 align:middle
Moreover, even after
the initial increase

01:39.706 --> 01:43.166 align:middle
in the target funds rate,
our policy is likely

01:43.196 --> 01:45.276 align:middle
to remain highly accommodative

01:45.866 --> 01:49.206 align:middle
to support continued
progress toward our objectives

01:49.586 --> 01:52.896 align:middle
of maximum employment
and 2 percent inflation.

01:53.336 --> 01:58.246 align:middle
I'll come back to today's policy
decisions in a few moments,

01:58.666 --> 02:01.926 align:middle
but first I would like to
review economic developments

02:01.926 --> 02:04.506 align:middle
and the outlook,
which formed the basis

02:04.506 --> 02:06.236 align:middle
for our policy decisions.

02:07.656 --> 02:12.356 align:middle
We have seen continued
progress toward our objective

02:12.356 --> 02:13.856 align:middle
of maximum employment.

02:14.956 --> 02:18.166 align:middle
The pace of employment
growth has remained strong,

02:18.746 --> 02:23.516 align:middle
with job gains averaging
nearly 290,000 per month

02:23.516 --> 02:25.126 align:middle
over the past three months.

02:26.246 --> 02:30.066 align:middle
The unemployment rate was
5.5 percent in February;

02:31.056 --> 02:34.986 align:middle
that's three-tenths lower than
the latest reading available

02:34.986 --> 02:36.876 align:middle
at the time of our
December meeting.

02:38.226 --> 02:41.766 align:middle
Broader measures of job
market conditions-such

02:41.766 --> 02:46.286 align:middle
as those counting individuals
who want and are available

02:46.346 --> 02:51.586 align:middle
to work but have not actively
searched recently and people

02:51.586 --> 02:53.176 align:middle
who are working part time

02:53.176 --> 02:56.736 align:middle
but would rather work
full time-have shown

02:56.736 --> 02:57.996 align:middle
similar improvement.

02:59.206 --> 03:00.996 align:middle
As we noted in our statement,

03:01.326 --> 03:04.516 align:middle
slack in the labor market
continues to diminish.

03:05.736 --> 03:09.326 align:middle
Meanwhile, the labor force
participation rate-the

03:09.326 --> 03:12.366 align:middle
percentage of working-age
Americans either working

03:12.366 --> 03:17.056 align:middle
or seeking work- is lower than
most estimates of its trend,

03:17.916 --> 03:20.216 align:middle
and wage growth remains
sluggish,

03:20.676 --> 03:24.036 align:middle
suggesting that some
cyclical weakness persists.

03:24.756 --> 03:28.616 align:middle
So considerable progress
clearly has been achieved,

03:29.346 --> 03:33.116 align:middle
but room for further improvement
in the labor market continues.

03:34.746 --> 03:38.766 align:middle
We continue to expect
sufficient underlying strength

03:39.246 --> 03:42.926 align:middle
in economic growth to
support ongoing improvement

03:42.926 --> 03:43.896 align:middle
in the labor market.

03:44.906 --> 03:49.196 align:middle
After averaging about 2 and
a half percent over 2014,

03:49.806 --> 03:53.786 align:middle
growth of real gross domestic
product appears to have slowed

03:53.996 --> 03:55.906 align:middle
in the first quarter
of this year,

03:56.656 --> 04:00.106 align:middle
in part reflecting a moderation
in household spending.

04:00.746 --> 04:06.316 align:middle
In addition, the recovery in the
housing sector remains subdued,

04:07.096 --> 04:09.616 align:middle
and export growth
looks to have weakened.

04:10.756 --> 04:13.546 align:middle
Looking ahead, however,
the Committee continues

04:13.546 --> 04:19.396 align:middle
to expect a moderate pace of GDP
growth, with robust job gains

04:19.586 --> 04:23.316 align:middle
and lower energy prices
supporting household spending.

04:24.786 --> 04:28.936 align:middle
Inflation has declined further
below our longer-run objective,

04:29.976 --> 04:34.016 align:middle
largely reflecting the lower
energy prices I just mentioned.

04:35.406 --> 04:39.306 align:middle
Declining import prices have
also restrained inflation,

04:40.176 --> 04:43.286 align:middle
and in light of the recent
appreciation of the dollar,

04:43.506 --> 04:46.696 align:middle
will likely continue to
do so in the months ahead.

04:47.996 --> 04:51.846 align:middle
My colleagues and I continue
to expect that as the effects

04:51.846 --> 04:54.236 align:middle
of these transitory
factors dissipate

04:55.066 --> 04:57.596 align:middle
and as the labor
market improves further,

04:58.296 --> 05:03.146 align:middle
inflation will move gradually
back toward our 2 percent

05:03.146 --> 05:05.326 align:middle
objective over the medium term.

05:06.536 --> 05:10.176 align:middle
In making this forecast, we
are attentive to the low levels

05:10.176 --> 05:13.386 align:middle
of market-based measures
of inflation compensation.

05:14.336 --> 05:16.886 align:middle
In contrast, survey-based
measures of longer-

05:16.886 --> 05:20.446 align:middle
term inflation expectations
have remained stable.

05:21.196 --> 05:23.026 align:middle
The Committee will continue

05:23.026 --> 05:25.836 align:middle
to monitor inflation
developments carefully.

05:27.036 --> 05:29.976 align:middle
This assessment of the
outlook is reflected

05:29.976 --> 05:33.886 align:middle
in the individual economic
projections submitted

05:33.886 --> 05:36.916 align:middle
for this meeting by
the FOMC participants.

05:37.626 --> 05:41.716 align:middle
As always, each participant's
projections are conditioned

05:41.716 --> 05:46.446 align:middle
on his or her own view of
appropriate monetary policy.

05:47.146 --> 05:49.886 align:middle
The unemployment
rate projections

05:50.126 --> 05:51.806 align:middle
over the next few years

05:52.306 --> 05:55.896 align:middle
and in the longer run
are generally a bit lower

05:55.896 --> 05:57.786 align:middle
than the December projections.

05:58.466 --> 06:01.496 align:middle
At the end of this year,
the central tendency

06:01.496 --> 06:06.296 align:middle
for the unemployment rate
stands at 5 to 5.2 percent,

06:06.776 --> 06:08.986 align:middle
in line with participants'
estimates

06:08.986 --> 06:11.386 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

06:12.486 --> 06:15.676 align:middle
Committee participants generally
see the unemployment rate

06:15.716 --> 06:21.056 align:middle
declining a little further over
the course of 2016 and 2017.

06:21.646 --> 06:23.386 align:middle
For economic growth,

06:23.466 --> 06:26.666 align:middle
participants generally
reduced their projections

06:26.946 --> 06:30.916 align:middle
since December, with many
citing a weaker outlook

06:30.916 --> 06:32.166 align:middle
for net exports.

06:32.996 --> 06:35.236 align:middle
Nonetheless, the
central tendency

06:35.236 --> 06:38.736 align:middle
of the growth projections
for this year and next,

06:39.316 --> 06:44.916 align:middle
at 2.3 to 2.7 percent, remains
somewhat above estimates

06:44.916 --> 06:47.066 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal growth rate.

06:48.086 --> 06:51.756 align:middle
Finally, FOMC participants
project inflation

06:51.756 --> 06:53.336 align:middle
to be quite low this year,

06:53.986 --> 06:57.326 align:middle
largely reflecting lower
energy and import prices.

06:58.386 --> 07:01.286 align:middle
The central tendency of
the inflation projections

07:01.286 --> 07:04.096 align:middle
for this year is
now below 1 percent,

07:04.806 --> 07:06.816 align:middle
down noticeably since December.

07:07.846 --> 07:11.536 align:middle
As the transitory factors
holding down inflation abate,

07:12.046 --> 07:18.066 align:middle
the central tendency rebounds
to 1.7 to 1.9 percent next year

07:18.776 --> 07:22.766 align:middle
and rises to 1.9 to
2 percent in 2017.

07:24.236 --> 07:27.936 align:middle
Returning to monetary policy,
as I noted at the outset,

07:28.276 --> 07:31.366 align:middle
the Committee reaffirmed
its view that the current 0

07:31.366 --> 07:34.056 align:middle
to one quarter percent
target range

07:34.056 --> 07:37.026 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate remains appropriate.

07:37.156 --> 07:40.316 align:middle
But with economic
conditions improving,

07:40.816 --> 07:44.346 align:middle
and with further improvement
expected in the months ahead,

07:44.976 --> 07:47.496 align:middle
we have again modified
our forward guidance.

07:48.296 --> 07:51.696 align:middle
In December and January,
the Committee judged

07:52.186 --> 07:54.156 align:middle
that it could be
patient in beginning

07:54.156 --> 07:57.346 align:middle
to normalize the stance
of monetary policy.

07:58.446 --> 08:01.346 align:middle
That meant that we
considered it unlikely

08:01.346 --> 08:04.706 align:middle
that economic conditions
would warrant an increase

08:04.706 --> 08:07.206 align:middle
in the target range for
the federal funds rate

08:07.406 --> 08:11.396 align:middle
for at least the next
couple of FOMC meetings.

08:12.486 --> 08:15.806 align:middle
While it is still the case
that we consider it unlikely

08:16.346 --> 08:19.136 align:middle
that economic conditions
will warrant an increase

08:19.136 --> 08:22.766 align:middle
in the target range
at the April meeting,

08:23.706 --> 08:27.446 align:middle
such an increase could be
warranted at any later meeting,

08:27.446 --> 08:29.746 align:middle
depending on how
the economy evolves.

08:30.846 --> 08:34.476 align:middle
Let me emphasize again
that today's modification

08:34.476 --> 08:38.586 align:middle
of the forward guidance should
not be read as indicating

08:38.586 --> 08:41.796 align:middle
that the Committee has
decided on the timing

08:41.796 --> 08:44.776 align:middle
of the initial increase
in the target range

08:44.836 --> 08:46.136 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

08:46.706 --> 08:49.876 align:middle
In particular, this
change does not mean

08:49.876 --> 08:53.606 align:middle
that an increase will
necessarily occur in June,

08:54.306 --> 08:56.256 align:middle
although we can't rule that out.

08:57.386 --> 09:00.436 align:middle
As we noted in our
statement, the decision

09:00.436 --> 09:04.656 align:middle
to raise the target range
will depend on our assessment

09:04.686 --> 09:09.196 align:middle
of realized and expected
progress toward our objectives

09:09.446 --> 09:12.496 align:middle
of maximum employment
and 2 percent inflation.

09:13.506 --> 09:16.676 align:middle
We continue to base that
assessment on a wide range

09:16.676 --> 09:19.086 align:middle
of information, including
measures

09:19.086 --> 09:20.786 align:middle
of labor market conditions,

09:21.086 --> 09:23.326 align:middle
indicators of inflation
pressures

09:23.366 --> 09:27.616 align:middle
and inflation expectations,
and readings on financial

09:27.616 --> 09:29.546 align:middle
and international developments.

09:30.796 --> 09:33.316 align:middle
We anticipate that it
will be appropriate

09:33.316 --> 09:36.396 align:middle
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate

09:37.086 --> 09:39.926 align:middle
when the Committee has
seen further improvement

09:40.156 --> 09:44.236 align:middle
in the labor market and
is reasonably confident

09:44.236 --> 09:46.716 align:middle
that inflation will move back

09:47.036 --> 09:50.066 align:middle
to its 2 percent objective
over the medium term.

09:51.176 --> 09:54.736 align:middle
Once we begin to remove
policy accommodation,

09:55.016 --> 10:00.476 align:middle
we continue to expect that-in
the words of our statement-"even

10:00.476 --> 10:03.866 align:middle
after employment and inflation
are near mandate-consistent

10:03.866 --> 10:07.756 align:middle
levels, economic conditions
may, for some time,

10:08.226 --> 10:12.196 align:middle
warrant keeping the target
federal funds rate below levels

10:12.246 --> 10:14.726 align:middle
the Committee views as
normal in the longer run."

10:16.006 --> 10:18.746 align:middle
This guidance is
consistent with the paths

10:18.746 --> 10:23.286 align:middle
for appropriate policy
reported by FOMC participants.

10:24.156 --> 10:26.946 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in December,

10:27.306 --> 10:31.566 align:middle
most participants lowered their
path for the federal funds rate,

10:32.166 --> 10:35.336 align:middle
consistent with the
downward revisions made

10:35.366 --> 10:40.616 align:middle
to the projections for GDP
growth and inflation as well

10:40.616 --> 10:42.316 align:middle
as somewhat lower estimates

10:42.316 --> 10:44.796 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

10:45.396 --> 10:50.306 align:middle
The median projection for the
federal funds rate is just below

10:50.306 --> 10:54.436 align:middle
2 percent in late
2016 and rises a bit

10:54.436 --> 10:57.386 align:middle
above 3 percent in late 2017.

10:58.456 --> 11:02.956 align:middle
The median projected rate
in 2017 remains below the

11:02.956 --> 11:08.566 align:middle
and three quarters percent or so
projected by most participants

11:08.566 --> 11:13.066 align:middle
as the rate's longer-run value,
even though the central tendency

11:13.066 --> 11:16.986 align:middle
of the unemployment rate by
that time is slightly below

11:16.986 --> 11:21.916 align:middle
that of its estimated longer-run
value, and the central tendency

11:21.916 --> 11:24.866 align:middle
for inflation is close to
our 2 percent objective.

11:26.146 --> 11:28.796 align:middle
Participants provide a
number of explanations

11:28.796 --> 11:32.316 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
running below its normal

11:32.546 --> 11:34.546 align:middle
longer-run level at that time.

11:35.406 --> 11:39.216 align:middle
These include, in particular,
the residual effects

11:39.216 --> 11:42.936 align:middle
of the financial crisis,
which are likely to continue

11:42.936 --> 11:47.286 align:middle
to constrain spending and credit
availability for some time.

11:47.856 --> 11:51.256 align:middle
I would like to emphasize
that these forecasts

11:51.256 --> 11:52.656 align:middle
of the appropriate path

11:52.656 --> 11:55.596 align:middle
of the federal funds
rate are conditional

11:55.596 --> 12:00.326 align:middle
on participants' individual
projections for economic output,

12:00.326 --> 12:02.336 align:middle
inflation, and other factors.

12:03.246 --> 12:05.306 align:middle
But our actual policy actions

12:05.306 --> 12:07.966 align:middle
over time will be
data dependent.

12:08.816 --> 12:12.886 align:middle
Accordingly, if the expansion
proves to be more vigorous

12:12.956 --> 12:17.026 align:middle
than currently anticipated
and inflation moves higher

12:17.076 --> 12:20.806 align:middle
than expected, then the
appropriate path would likely

12:20.806 --> 12:25.236 align:middle
follow a steeper and higher
trajectory; conversely,

12:25.236 --> 12:27.756 align:middle
if conditions were
to prove weaker,

12:28.196 --> 12:31.976 align:middle
then the appropriate trajectory
would be lower and less steep.

12:32.586 --> 12:36.186 align:middle
Finally, the Committee
will continue its policy

12:36.186 --> 12:40.646 align:middle
of reinvesting proceeds from
maturing Treasury securities

12:40.996 --> 12:43.536 align:middle
and principal payments
from agency debt

12:44.006 --> 12:45.826 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities.

12:46.476 --> 12:48.326 align:middle
The Committee's sizable holdings

12:48.326 --> 12:52.676 align:middle
of longer-term securities should
help maintain accommodative

12:52.676 --> 12:56.446 align:middle
financial conditions and
promote further progress toward

12:56.446 --> 12:57.366 align:middle
our objectives.

12:57.946 --> 12:58.896 align:middle
Thank you.

12:59.326 --> 13:01.876 align:middle
Now I'd be happy to
take your questions.

13:03.906 --> 13:06.396 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

13:07.996 --> 13:09.206 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

13:09.206 --> 13:09.876 align:middle
Hi, thank you.

13:10.326 --> 13:13.116 align:middle
So there's been this
pretty consistent reference

13:13.116 --> 13:16.136 align:middle
to expectations of above-trend
growth over the last few months.

13:16.136 --> 13:19.546 align:middle
Now we're seeing growth
downgraded in the context

13:19.546 --> 13:21.786 align:middle
of very explicit
references to international

13:21.786 --> 13:24.506 align:middle
and external conditions: weak
export growth, oil dragging

13:24.506 --> 13:26.946 align:middle
down inflation, and your own
comments now on the dollar.

13:27.066 --> 13:29.296 align:middle
So my question is,
doesn't this indicate

13:29.296 --> 13:31.586 align:middle
that the Fed's facing a
tougher time-you know,

13:31.586 --> 13:33.586 align:middle
kind of going it alone,
decoupling from the rest

13:33.586 --> 13:36.516 align:middle
of the world-than perhaps
you expected last fall

13:36.516 --> 13:37.966 align:middle
when this first started
to be an issue?

13:38.686 --> 13:39.126 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

13:39.606 --> 13:44.736 align:middle
Well, it looks like, from
incoming data pertaining

13:44.736 --> 13:50.096 align:middle
to the first quarter, that real
GDP growth has declined somewhat

13:50.386 --> 13:55.546 align:middle
below where it was for the last
several quarters of last year.

13:55.936 --> 13:59.676 align:middle
And that's really why
the Committee indicated

13:59.676 --> 14:03.156 align:middle
that growth has moderated
somewhat.

14:03.326 --> 14:08.646 align:middle
There has been a slight
downgrading of estimates

14:08.646 --> 14:10.866 align:middle
of growth for this year.

14:12.136 --> 14:13.806 align:middle
You mentioned the dollar.

14:14.276 --> 14:18.096 align:middle
We noted that export
growth has weakened.

14:18.516 --> 14:24.276 align:middle
Probably the strong dollar
is one reason for that.

14:24.596 --> 14:28.096 align:middle
On the other hand, the strength
of the dollar also, in part,

14:28.096 --> 14:31.616 align:middle
reflects the strength
of the U.S. economy.

14:32.316 --> 14:37.276 align:middle
The strength of the dollar
is also one factor that,

14:37.276 --> 14:40.496 align:middle
as I noted, is holding
down import prices and,

14:40.496 --> 14:43.926 align:middle
at least on a transitory
basis at this point,

14:44.006 --> 14:46.266 align:middle
pushing inflation down.

14:46.596 --> 14:51.006 align:middle
So, we are taking account of
international developments,

14:51.356 --> 14:54.666 align:middle
including prospects for
growth in our trade partners,

14:55.056 --> 14:57.456 align:middle
in making the forecast
we have here.

14:57.906 --> 15:01.446 align:middle
Nevertheless, it is
important to recognize

15:01.476 --> 15:03.706 align:middle
that this is not
a weak forecast.

15:04.196 --> 15:06.326 align:middle
Taking everything into account,

15:06.746 --> 15:10.636 align:middle
we continue to project
above-trend growth.

15:11.086 --> 15:14.236 align:middle
We continue to project
improvement

15:14.236 --> 15:18.016 align:middle
in the labor market
by the end of 2015.

15:18.356 --> 15:23.126 align:middle
The central tendency of the
participants is, they're looking

15:23.206 --> 15:27.986 align:middle
for an unemployment rate that
will be down to 5.0 to 5.2,

15:27.986 --> 15:31.456 align:middle
which is consistent
with their estimates

15:31.456 --> 15:33.746 align:middle
of its longer-run normal value.

15:33.746 --> 15:38.466 align:middle
So, we do see considerable
underlying strength

15:38.706 --> 15:40.256 align:middle
in the U.S. economy.

15:40.626 --> 15:45.736 align:middle
And, in spite of what looks
like a weaker first quarter,

15:45.996 --> 15:49.576 align:middle
we are projecting good
performance for the economy.

15:50.726 --> 15:55.466 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

15:55.466 --> 15:57.216 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

15:57.546 --> 16:00.476 align:middle
The policy statement
today talks about one

16:00.476 --> 16:03.616 align:middle
of the prerequisites you'll need
to start raising rates is to be

16:03.616 --> 16:04.836 align:middle
"reasonably confident"

16:05.176 --> 16:08.666 align:middle
that inflation-your inflation
target-will be met at 2 percent,

16:09.076 --> 16:10.876 align:middle
but that is coming out at a time

16:10.876 --> 16:13.916 align:middle
when you've lowered your
forecast on inflation,

16:14.306 --> 16:18.156 align:middle
what-which I would think
would make you less confident

16:18.486 --> 16:19.106 align:middle
about it.

16:19.386 --> 16:21.156 align:middle
What is it going
to take to make you

16:21.156 --> 16:22.956 align:middle
"reasonably confident"
about inflation?

16:22.956 --> 16:23.526 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

16:23.526 --> 16:26.106 align:middle
So, I don't have a
mechanical answer for you.

16:26.106 --> 16:31.466 align:middle
There is no single
thing where I'd say,

16:31.466 --> 16:34.986 align:middle
"We must see such-and-such
in order to achieve

16:35.026 --> 16:36.996 align:middle
that level of confidence."

16:37.136 --> 16:40.756 align:middle
We will be looking at
a wide array of data.

16:41.726 --> 16:43.996 align:middle
Now, we've said that
we also want

16:44.096 --> 16:47.546 align:middle
to see continued improvement
in the labor market.

16:47.996 --> 16:50.146 align:middle
And a stronger labor market

16:50.146 --> 16:55.676 align:middle
with less labor market slack is
one factor that would tend to,

16:56.136 --> 16:58.996 align:middle
certainly for me,
increase my confidence

16:59.376 --> 17:01.016 align:middle
that as slack diminishes,

17:01.256 --> 17:03.816 align:middle
that inflation will
move up over time.

17:04.196 --> 17:07.446 align:middle
Other things I will be
looking at-of course,

17:07.476 --> 17:13.096 align:middle
the inflation data, but as
we said, we expect inflation

17:13.096 --> 17:17.886 align:middle
to remain quite low because
of the depressing influence

17:18.146 --> 17:22.046 align:middle
of energy price declines
and the dollar.

17:22.526 --> 17:29.316 align:middle
But we will be looking at the
inflation data carefully to see

17:29.316 --> 17:31.806 align:middle
if we can interpret,
for example,

17:32.066 --> 17:37.296 align:middle
low levels of inflation-if
we see that, which we expect,

17:37.296 --> 17:39.716 align:middle
as reflecting those influences.

17:40.156 --> 17:43.296 align:middle
We will be looking
at wage growth.

17:43.296 --> 17:45.726 align:middle
We have not seen
wage growth pick up.

17:46.076 --> 17:49.716 align:middle
We may not see wage growth
pick up, I wouldn't say either

17:49.716 --> 17:53.536 align:middle
that that is a precondition
to raising rates.

17:53.796 --> 17:57.816 align:middle
But if we did see wage
growth pick up, that would be

17:57.816 --> 17:58.936 align:middle
at least a symptom

17:58.936 --> 18:02.496 align:middle
that inflation would
likely move up over time.

18:02.496 --> 18:05.926 align:middle
We'll be watching
inflation expectations.

18:06.186 --> 18:08.186 align:middle
Survey measures have
been stable;

18:08.406 --> 18:10.276 align:middle
I expect that to continue,

18:10.616 --> 18:13.266 align:middle
but we will be watching
it carefully.

18:13.646 --> 18:15.606 align:middle
And market-based measures

18:15.606 --> 18:18.606 align:middle
of inflation compensation
have fallen.

18:18.606 --> 18:19.386 align:middle
They're low.

18:20.026 --> 18:24.106 align:middle
If they were to move up over
time, that would probably serve

18:24.176 --> 18:26.506 align:middle
to increase my confidence.

18:26.606 --> 18:28.826 align:middle
But there are a wide
range of things

18:29.246 --> 18:31.516 align:middle
that we will be looking at,

18:31.716 --> 18:34.466 align:middle
including further improvement
in the labor market.

18:34.466 --> 18:36.016 align:middle
So there's no simple answer.

18:36.506 --> 18:39.686 align:middle
This is a judgment that the
Committee will have to make.

18:40.796 --> 18:45.596 align:middle
JON HILSENRATH.

18:45.596 --> 18:47.836 align:middle
Jon Hilsenrath from the
Wall Street Journal.

18:47.956 --> 18:51.656 align:middle
Chair Yellen, the famous
dot plot that we always talk

18:51.656 --> 18:54.826 align:middle
about showed that
officials' expectations

18:54.826 --> 18:57.966 align:middle
for where interest rates are
going to end the year in 2015,

18:57.966 --> 19:01.166 align:middle
'16, and '17 have come
down fairly notably.

19:01.166 --> 19:05.556 align:middle
I wonder if you could
explain to us your analysis

19:05.556 --> 19:07.836 align:middle
of why those estimates
are coming down.

19:08.326 --> 19:12.676 align:middle
And, specifically, is it a
reflection of-what's changed?

19:12.676 --> 19:15.816 align:middle
And is it a reflection
of the changes

19:15.816 --> 19:18.536 align:middle
in the Fed's economic
forecast or a change

19:18.536 --> 19:21.456 align:middle
in the way the Fed is reacting
to the economy that it sees-

19:21.456 --> 19:22.526 align:middle
its reaction function?

19:24.016 --> 19:24.646 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

19:24.966 --> 19:30.756 align:middle
So, it's always hard to know
exactly why each participant has

19:31.046 --> 19:33.666 align:middle
written down the
forecast they have,

19:34.096 --> 19:39.816 align:middle
but certainly there are
changes in the assessments

19:39.816 --> 19:44.446 align:middle
of the economy and forecasts
for the economy that would point

19:44.446 --> 19:46.806 align:middle
in the direction of
downward adjustment

19:47.116 --> 19:48.566 align:middle
in the funds rate path.

19:48.566 --> 19:54.076 align:middle
For one thing, you do see
meaningful downward adjustment

19:54.076 --> 19:58.646 align:middle
in the inflation forecast,
certainly for this year.

19:59.656 --> 20:01.646 align:middle
In addition, importantly,

20:02.486 --> 20:07.906 align:middle
a number of participants have
marked down their estimates

20:07.956 --> 20:12.306 align:middle
of the normal longer-run
unemployment rate.

20:12.306 --> 20:16.686 align:middle
So that range has moved
down noticeably from-

20:17.046 --> 20:20.086 align:middle
previously it was 5.2 to 5.5,

20:20.476 --> 20:24.746 align:middle
and it's now moved
down to 5.0 to 5.2.

20:25.146 --> 20:26.926 align:middle
And downward revisions

20:26.956 --> 20:31.116 align:middle
to the longer-run
normal unemployment rate

20:31.116 --> 20:34.026 align:middle
in a way suggests

20:34.026 --> 20:38.176 align:middle
that participants are seeing
more slack in the economy now

20:38.266 --> 20:40.666 align:middle
than they previously did.

20:40.666 --> 20:45.736 align:middle
So I think both of those things
would point to downward revision

20:45.736 --> 20:49.346 align:middle
in the funds rate path.

20:49.646 --> 20:51.466 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

20:51.466 --> 20:53.226 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

20:53.756 --> 20:58.436 align:middle
The experience of some other
central banks-notably Japan,

20:58.776 --> 21:03.616 align:middle
Sweden also-suggests that
tightening early when you're

21:03.616 --> 21:07.056 align:middle
at the zero lower bound can
be a risky process-the risks

21:07.056 --> 21:09.976 align:middle
of tightening early can
dramatically outweigh the risks

21:09.976 --> 21:11.896 align:middle
of leaving things
a little longer.

21:11.896 --> 21:15.376 align:middle
I wonder if you could comment
on that international experience

21:15.376 --> 21:18.296 align:middle
and explain how that's
influencing the debate

21:18.296 --> 21:19.426 align:middle
in the FOMC at the moment.

21:19.826 --> 21:20.956 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

21:21.126 --> 21:23.916 align:middle
Well, when an economy
is operating

21:23.916 --> 21:26.376 align:middle
at the so-called
zero lower bound,

21:27.626 --> 21:30.436 align:middle
there-it creates a situation

21:30.436 --> 21:33.076 align:middle
where there are asymmetric
risks.

21:33.286 --> 21:37.546 align:middle
It is possible, if the
economy proves stronger

21:37.546 --> 21:42.386 align:middle
than is expected, to respond
to that by tightening policy.

21:42.386 --> 21:47.346 align:middle
If there are adverse
shocks to demand that tend

21:47.346 --> 21:50.736 align:middle
to push inflation and
economic performance

21:51.346 --> 21:55.806 align:middle
in an adverse direction, it's
not possible to lower rates.

21:55.806 --> 21:59.856 align:middle
Of course, that's a reason
why, for a number of years,

21:59.856 --> 22:03.586 align:middle
we engaged in active
asset purchase programs.

22:03.586 --> 22:08.836 align:middle
So there is a situation
there of asymmetric risks,

22:08.876 --> 22:11.146 align:middle
and it does point
in the direction

22:11.376 --> 22:15.186 align:middle
of waiting longer
to raise rates.

22:15.186 --> 22:19.406 align:middle
But I would say that this is
an influence that we-and a set

22:19.406 --> 22:22.846 align:middle
of considerations-that we
have long been aware of

22:22.846 --> 22:25.276 align:middle
and have been taking
into account,

22:25.486 --> 22:29.616 align:middle
so that it's not something
that just comes into play now.

22:29.846 --> 22:35.486 align:middle
It is a reason that we
have held our rates at 0

22:35.486 --> 22:40.496 align:middle
to one quarter percent for,
now, roughly six years.

22:41.026 --> 22:45.136 align:middle
So, we are seeing an economy
that's growing above trend,

22:45.256 --> 22:47.356 align:middle
the labor market is improving.

22:47.666 --> 22:49.416 align:middle
I think some of the headwinds

22:49.416 --> 22:54.716 align:middle
that have long been holding
the economy back are beginning

22:54.716 --> 22:59.096 align:middle
to recede, which is a reason
that the Committee wants

22:59.096 --> 23:02.826 align:middle
to be able to evaluate
incoming data and consider

23:02.826 --> 23:07.276 align:middle
when it may be appropriate
to finally raise rates.

23:07.306 --> 23:16.746 align:middle
But that is a consideration we
have long taken into account.

23:17.166 --> 23:17.886 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

23:17.886 --> 23:19.446 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

23:19.446 --> 23:22.166 align:middle
I don't hear, Chair Yellen,
any quantitative measures

23:22.166 --> 23:24.936 align:middle
of what increasing
confidence is in inflation

23:25.476 --> 23:28.586 align:middle
or heading back towards-or
a further improvement

23:28.586 --> 23:30.626 align:middle
in the job market, which
is unusual for a Fed

23:30.626 --> 23:34.036 align:middle
that not too long ago
was providing us metrics

23:34.036 --> 23:35.936 align:middle
on unemployment about when
it would move with rates.

23:36.456 --> 23:39.966 align:middle
Is it now policy to keep
the market guessing?

23:40.446 --> 23:43.026 align:middle
And is it thought that
you'd have better policy

23:43.056 --> 23:45.806 align:middle
and economic outcomes
from less certainty

23:45.806 --> 23:47.356 align:middle
about the path of
interest rates?

23:47.816 --> 23:49.676 align:middle
And a kind of related
question, if you will:

23:50.016 --> 23:54.576 align:middle
Could you see raising rates
while the Committee still judges

23:54.926 --> 23:56.006 align:middle
that the risks are balanced?

23:56.486 --> 23:59.396 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

23:59.536 --> 24:03.836 align:middle
So, in terms of certainty
and providing metrics,

24:05.736 --> 24:08.996 align:middle
we provided a metric
or a threshold of 6

24:08.996 --> 24:15.086 align:middle
and a half percent several years
ago and told market participants

24:15.086 --> 24:19.996 align:middle
and the public that we
wouldn't consider it appropriate

24:20.726 --> 24:26.196 align:middle
to raise rates as long as the
unemployment rate was higher

24:26.196 --> 24:31.626 align:middle
than that level, as long as
inflation was well contained.

24:32.556 --> 24:39.266 align:middle
But our policy needs
to be data dependent,

24:40.046 --> 24:45.426 align:middle
and we need to respond to
incoming data and our assessment

24:45.426 --> 24:47.856 align:middle
of incoming data in terms

24:48.566 --> 24:51.306 align:middle
of where we think the
economy is heading

24:51.866 --> 24:55.166 align:middle
and how close we are
to our objectives.

24:55.896 --> 25:00.296 align:middle
And the markets-so, can
we provide certainty?

25:00.496 --> 25:02.636 align:middle
Of course we can't
provide certainty

25:02.636 --> 25:05.746 align:middle
because we're not certain
what the data will look like

25:05.746 --> 25:08.216 align:middle
and how the economy will evolve.

25:08.686 --> 25:12.496 align:middle
And to achieve our objectives,
we need to watch the data,

25:12.496 --> 25:18.336 align:middle
continually reformulate our
best guesses-our forecasts

25:18.336 --> 25:22.786 align:middle
of where the economy is
going-and respond appropriately.

25:23.346 --> 25:27.496 align:middle
And we can't provide certainty
and shouldn't provide certainty

25:27.856 --> 25:32.116 align:middle
because economic developments
that will unfold are uncertain.

25:32.536 --> 25:36.456 align:middle
And what market participants
should be doing is looking

25:36.456 --> 25:41.466 align:middle
at incoming data just as we are
and forming their expectations

25:41.546 --> 25:46.876 align:middle
for where policy will be going
and should be going just exactly

25:46.876 --> 25:49.786 align:middle
as we will be doing
by attempting

25:49.826 --> 25:55.646 align:middle
to understand economic
developments as they unfold.

25:55.646 --> 26:00.206 align:middle
And that's-that is what we're
trying to say in this statement,

26:00.206 --> 26:03.146 align:middle
that that's what we will
be doing going forward.

26:03.436 --> 26:06.306 align:middle
And we don't want to-and
don't think it's appropriate

26:06.646 --> 26:10.276 align:middle
at this point-to provide
calendar-based guidance.

26:10.696 --> 26:11.026 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

26:11.146 --> 26:15.306 align:middle
Can I have the second
part about the balance

26:15.426 --> 26:21.796 align:middle
of risks-would you raise
rates when risks are balanced?

26:21.796 --> 26:23.246 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

26:23.246 --> 26:23.926 align:middle
The risks to what?

26:23.926 --> 26:25.146 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

26:25.146 --> 26:28.776 align:middle
If risks are balanced, as
they say in the statement,

26:28.776 --> 26:30.156 align:middle
could you be raising
rates in that context?

26:30.156 --> 26:30.696 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

26:30.696 --> 26:32.556 align:middle
Well, I guess we said the risks
to the outlook are balanced.

26:32.556 --> 26:35.896 align:middle
And, I mean, certainly we could
raise rates in a situation

26:36.106 --> 26:37.426 align:middle
where the risks are balanced.

26:37.426 --> 26:39.706 align:middle
We need to see, as we have said,

26:39.976 --> 26:43.196 align:middle
we want to see further
improvement in the labor market,

26:43.196 --> 26:55.676 align:middle
and we want to feel reasonably
confident that the economy is

26:55.736 --> 26:59.506 align:middle
on a trajectory where we
will achieve our 2 percent

26:59.596 --> 27:01.256 align:middle
inflation objective.

27:01.326 --> 27:02.706 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

27:02.776 --> 27:04.226 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum, New York Times.

27:04.226 --> 27:06.716 align:middle
There seems to be an awful
lot riding on surveys

27:06.716 --> 27:08.326 align:middle
of inflation expectations,

27:08.326 --> 27:10.936 align:middle
yet those surveys are
an imprecise instrument;

27:10.936 --> 27:14.086 align:middle
they don't seem particularly
sensitive to the types

27:14.206 --> 27:19.726 align:middle
of changes in inflation that
we have seen in recent years,

27:19.726 --> 27:22.076 align:middle
they don't seem to
differentiate between, say,

27:22.076 --> 27:25.726 align:middle
1.5 and 2-the expectations
remain stable even

27:25.726 --> 27:26.346 align:middle
through those changes.

27:26.346 --> 27:29.146 align:middle
Could you talk a little bit

27:29.406 --> 27:32.016 align:middle
about why the FOMC
has confidence

27:32.016 --> 27:35.596 align:middle
that those measures are
accurate reflections

27:36.006 --> 27:42.606 align:middle
of where inflation is likely
to go and whether, you know,

27:42.606 --> 27:45.106 align:middle
the concerns that
you've articulated

27:45.106 --> 27:49.386 align:middle
about market-based measures
at all correspond to concerns

27:49.386 --> 27:52.556 align:middle
that you may have about
survey-based measures?

27:52.556 --> 27:53.296 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

27:53.296 --> 27:55.086 align:middle
Well, survey-based
measures aren't perfect.

27:55.086 --> 27:58.426 align:middle
And the mean-often the
mean or even the median

27:58.426 --> 28:00.816 align:middle
of those measures does
not line up very well

28:00.856 --> 28:04.246 align:middle
with actual inflation, so
they seem to be biased.

28:04.246 --> 28:07.106 align:middle
Nevertheless, they
do seem to be useful

28:07.206 --> 28:10.136 align:middle
in predicting actual
movements in inflation.

28:10.486 --> 28:16.756 align:middle
And because we think inflation
expectations are a determinant

28:17.366 --> 28:22.466 align:middle
of price setting, we need to
be looking at the best data

28:22.466 --> 28:25.076 align:middle
that we can, even
if it's imperfect,

28:25.076 --> 28:29.416 align:middle
in trying to gauge
inflation expectations.

28:29.416 --> 28:31.946 align:middle
And so we do look
at survey measures.

28:31.946 --> 28:33.906 align:middle
Now, the fact that survey
measures are stable,

28:33.906 --> 28:37.976 align:middle
even if they are stable
at levels consistent

28:37.976 --> 28:43.746 align:middle
with inflation objectives that
a central bank wants to achieve,

28:43.746 --> 28:46.036 align:middle
that's not a guarantee that
inflation will, over time,

28:46.036 --> 28:49.726 align:middle
move to be consistent
with those expectations.

28:49.726 --> 28:52.226 align:middle
An example is Japan,
I would give you,

28:52.226 --> 28:53.866 align:middle
where for many years
the households

28:53.866 --> 28:56.706 align:middle
and businesses expected
positive inflation

28:56.706 --> 28:59.286 align:middle
but there was a consistent
undershoot.

28:59.286 --> 29:03.666 align:middle
So, this isn't a single metric
that is perfect, but it's one

29:04.326 --> 29:05.886 align:middle
of many things we'd look at.

29:05.886 --> 29:09.036 align:middle
We also look at measures of
inflation expectations based

29:09.036 --> 29:12.916 align:middle
on market differentials between
nominal and real or TIPS yields.

29:12.916 --> 29:14.826 align:middle
They're also informative
but can move

29:14.826 --> 29:19.216 align:middle
around for reasons pertaining to
liquidity in the Treasury market

29:19.216 --> 29:21.866 align:middle
and in the TIPS market, and also

29:21.866 --> 29:25.566 align:middle
because of changing
perceptions of inflation risk.

29:25.866 --> 29:30.046 align:middle
So, they're not a
pure read, either.

29:30.046 --> 29:32.816 align:middle
And we want to look
at both things

29:32.816 --> 29:35.266 align:middle
and not take away
any simple morals.

29:36.756 --> 29:38.506 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

29:39.456 --> 29:40.886 align:middle
Peter Barnes, Fox Business.

29:41.696 --> 29:44.316 align:middle
Chair Yellen, I wanted to check
in again with you on whether

29:44.646 --> 29:48.666 align:middle
or not you see or have any
concerns about bubbles out there

29:48.666 --> 29:50.836 align:middle
in the economy, particularly
the financial markets-debt

29:50.836 --> 29:52.506 align:middle
and equity markets.

29:52.506 --> 29:54.786 align:middle
And I wanted to refer to your
most recent Monetary Policy

29:54.786 --> 29:57.516 align:middle
Report to Congress last
month, in which you said

29:57.516 --> 29:58.956 align:middle
"overall equity valuations

29:58.956 --> 30:02.326 align:middle
by some conventional
measures are somewhat higher

30:02.326 --> 30:03.566 align:middle
than their historical levels .

30:03.566 --> 30:06.316 align:middle
. . valuation metrics in
some sectors continued

30:06.316 --> 30:10.156 align:middle
to appear stretched relative
to historical norms."

30:10.746 --> 30:13.036 align:middle
In the same report
last year, in July,

30:13.156 --> 30:16.606 align:middle
the report specifically
mentioned biotech

30:16.676 --> 30:18.636 align:middle
and social media stocks as being

30:18.636 --> 30:22.516 align:middle
"substantially"-let me see
here-"substantially stretched."

30:23.936 --> 30:27.506 align:middle
Do you still feel that way
and can you comment on bubbles

30:27.506 --> 30:28.646 align:middle
and particularly these sectors?

30:28.726 --> 30:29.246 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

30:30.076 --> 30:33.886 align:middle
Well, I don't want to comment
on those particular sectors.

30:35.646 --> 30:40.146 align:middle
You know, as we said in the
report, overall measures

30:40.146 --> 30:42.826 align:middle
of equity valuations
are on the high side

30:42.866 --> 30:47.386 align:middle
but not outside of
historical ranges.

30:48.566 --> 30:52.996 align:middle
In some corporate debt
markets, we do see evidence

30:52.996 --> 30:56.386 align:middle
of unusually low spreads,

30:56.676 --> 30:59.516 align:middle
and that's what was
referred to in the report.

30:59.886 --> 31:05.496 align:middle
More broadly, we do try to
assess potential threats

31:05.496 --> 31:07.246 align:middle
to financial stability.

31:07.616 --> 31:12.286 align:middle
And in addition to looking at
asset valuations, we also look

31:12.286 --> 31:17.106 align:middle
at measures of credit
growth, of the extent

31:17.106 --> 31:20.436 align:middle
of leverage being
used in the economy

31:20.436 --> 31:23.466 align:middle
and in the financial
sector, and the extent

31:23.466 --> 31:25.616 align:middle
of maturity transformation.

31:26.096 --> 31:31.586 align:middle
And taking into account a broad
range of metrics that bear

31:31.586 --> 31:35.036 align:middle
on financial stability,
our overall assessment

31:35.036 --> 31:37.346 align:middle
at this point is that
threats are moderate.

31:38.596 --> 31:42.286 align:middle
JENNIFER LIBERTO.

31:42.866 --> 31:45.486 align:middle
Jen Liberto, Politico.

31:45.486 --> 31:48.476 align:middle
So, I want to switch gears a
little bit and ask about some

31:48.576 --> 31:51.656 align:middle
of the tension between the
Fed and Congress lately,

31:51.656 --> 31:54.486 align:middle
with some lawmakers calling
for more transparency

31:54.486 --> 31:55.656 align:middle
and accountability measures.

31:56.216 --> 31:58.806 align:middle
And I wanted to ask to what
degree that there might be room

31:58.916 --> 32:03.886 align:middle
for the Fed to consider
some of these measures,

32:03.886 --> 32:06.246 align:middle
like maybe a rules-based
approach like the Taylor rule,

32:06.246 --> 32:08.006 align:middle
or some of these
measures that would change

32:08.006 --> 32:12.276 align:middle
up who has a voting
seat on the FOMC.

32:12.816 --> 32:14.616 align:middle
To what degree does that
make it more difficult

32:14.616 --> 32:15.576 align:middle
to accomplish your mission?

32:16.106 --> 32:16.956 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

32:17.126 --> 32:20.946 align:middle
So, I believe the Federal
Reserve is already one

32:20.946 --> 32:25.786 align:middle
of the most transparent central
banks of any around the globe.

32:26.146 --> 32:29.466 align:middle
We provide an immense
amount of information,

32:29.496 --> 32:35.036 align:middle
both financial-about our
balance sheet-and our monetary

32:35.036 --> 32:36.636 align:middle
policy operations.

32:36.946 --> 32:39.286 align:middle
We have audited financial
statements.

32:39.586 --> 32:42.586 align:middle
We publish our balance
sheet every week.

32:42.726 --> 32:46.176 align:middle
If you want to know exactly
what's in the SOMA portfolio,

32:46.176 --> 32:49.156 align:middle
it's listed on the
New York Fed website

32:49.366 --> 32:51.526 align:middle
on a CUSIP-by-CUSIP basis.

32:51.846 --> 32:53.336 align:middle
I have press conferences.

32:53.336 --> 32:54.726 align:middle
We issue minutes.

32:55.416 --> 33:00.166 align:middle
We have, you know, statements
that we release right

33:00.166 --> 33:03.956 align:middle
after meetings and
transcripts within five years.

33:03.956 --> 33:06.016 align:middle
So, if you put all
that together,

33:06.236 --> 33:09.536 align:middle
we are a transparent
central bank.

33:10.716 --> 33:16.186 align:middle
With respect to congressional
changes that are

33:16.186 --> 33:21.666 align:middle
under consideration that would
politicize monetary policy

33:22.516 --> 33:29.496 align:middle
by bringing Congress in to
make policy judgments about,

33:29.496 --> 33:33.346 align:middle
in real-time, on our
monetary policy decisions.

33:33.936 --> 33:40.216 align:middle
Congress itself decided in 1978
that that was a bad thing to do,

33:40.216 --> 33:43.836 align:middle
that it would lead to
poor economic performance,

33:43.906 --> 33:49.146 align:middle
and they carved out this
one area of policy reviews,

33:49.146 --> 33:55.236 align:middle
of monetary policy
decisionmaking, from GAO audits.

33:55.306 --> 33:59.246 align:middle
The GAO looks at everything else
that goes on within the Fed.

33:59.636 --> 34:04.236 align:middle
And I think that that is a
central bank best practice.

34:04.326 --> 34:06.586 align:middle
The global experience shows

34:06.886 --> 34:09.966 align:middle
that giving central
banks independence

34:09.996 --> 34:14.826 align:middle
to make monetary policy
decisions that they think are

34:14.826 --> 34:17.946 align:middle
in the best interest of
the country and consistent

34:17.946 --> 34:21.346 align:middle
with their mandates
leads to lower inflation

34:21.556 --> 34:24.306 align:middle
and more stable macroeconomic
outcomes.

34:24.306 --> 34:27.936 align:middle
So, I feel very strongly
about that,

34:28.176 --> 34:30.916 align:middle
but we are accountable
to Congress.

34:30.916 --> 34:35.276 align:middle
Of course, we're ready
to provide information

34:35.276 --> 34:39.966 align:middle
that Congress needs to evaluate
the Fed's decisionmaking,

34:40.356 --> 34:42.606 align:middle
in monetary policy
and elsewhere.

34:43.866 --> 34:50.826 align:middle
With respect to monetary policy
rules, they can be useful,

34:50.826 --> 34:55.686 align:middle
and I find them useful-and long
have-as a kind of benchmark

34:55.806 --> 34:59.246 align:middle
for thinking about what might
be the appropriate stance

34:59.246 --> 35:00.066 align:middle
of policy.

35:00.586 --> 35:02.856 align:middle
But to chain a central bank

35:02.956 --> 35:08.516 align:middle
to follow a simple mathematical
rule that fails to take account

35:08.696 --> 35:12.976 align:middle
of many things that
are very important

35:12.976 --> 35:18.046 align:middle
in making monetary policy-for
example, I was earlier asked

35:18.046 --> 35:21.246 align:middle
about being against
the zero lower bound,

35:21.556 --> 35:24.736 align:middle
which is an important
special consideration-

35:25.656 --> 35:28.796 align:middle
that would be a very foolish
thing to do, and I oppose it.

35:29.286 --> 35:32.576 align:middle
With respect to proposals
having to do with voting

35:32.926 --> 35:35.516 align:middle
and the structure of the
Fed that you mentioned,

35:35.516 --> 35:37.816 align:middle
a lot of ideas have
been mentioned.

35:38.396 --> 35:40.166 align:middle
I would say, for my part,

35:40.566 --> 35:44.806 align:middle
I think the Federal
Reserve works well.

35:45.356 --> 35:51.866 align:middle
The system we have was put into
place by Congress decades ago.

35:52.256 --> 35:54.676 align:middle
I don't think it's a
system that's broken.

35:55.036 --> 35:59.416 align:middle
Of course, Congress can
revisit the decisions it's made

35:59.416 --> 36:01.196 align:middle
about the structure of the Fed.

36:01.656 --> 36:05.106 align:middle
There were good reasons
for making the decisions

36:05.106 --> 36:07.746 align:middle
that were made about
how to structure voting

36:07.746 --> 36:14.106 align:middle
and other things, but-and
I don't think the system is

36:14.196 --> 36:16.296 align:middle
broken, I think it's
working well.

36:16.926 --> 36:18.856 align:middle
So I don't see a
need for changes.

36:18.906 --> 36:23.346 align:middle
But, of course, it's up to
Congress to review that.

36:26.326 --> 36:28.186 align:middle
STEVEN MUFSON.

36:28.186 --> 36:32.466 align:middle
I was wondering whether you
could quantify the effect

36:32.616 --> 36:36.406 align:middle
that the stronger dollar
has had on economic output

36:36.406 --> 36:39.216 align:middle
so far this year; the extent
to which it's sort of acted

36:39.216 --> 36:42.736 align:middle
as its own rate increase;
and what sort

36:42.736 --> 36:47.356 align:middle
of obligation you feel, if any,
to make life easier for the ECB,

36:47.396 --> 36:50.166 align:middle
Bank of Japan, and the many
emerging market countries

36:50.166 --> 36:53.616 align:middle
that are struggling with some
of the issues we struggled

36:53.616 --> 36:54.696 align:middle
with not that long ago.

36:55.236 --> 36:56.236 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:56.376 --> 36:59.146 align:middle
So, with respect to the
impact of the dollar

36:59.146 --> 37:03.656 align:middle
on the U.S. economy, I don't
have a quantitative estimate

37:03.726 --> 37:09.846 align:middle
to offer you, but I certainly
expect net exports to serve

37:09.846 --> 37:12.946 align:middle
as a notable drag, this
year, on the outlook.

37:13.396 --> 37:18.606 align:middle
But, remember, we have to put
that in context: There are a lot

37:18.606 --> 37:20.966 align:middle
of things that affect
the U.S. outlook,

37:21.286 --> 37:28.226 align:middle
and while that is serving as
a drag on economic growth,

37:28.466 --> 37:30.766 align:middle
overall the Committee continues

37:30.766 --> 37:34.106 align:middle
to see sufficient
strength-particularly

37:34.106 --> 37:40.626 align:middle
in private spending-that we are
expecting above-trend growth,

37:40.626 --> 37:41.456 align:middle
even so.

37:42.106 --> 37:50.006 align:middle
With respect to our neighbors,
we look very carefully

37:50.006 --> 37:52.936 align:middle
at what's happening in
the global environment.

37:53.276 --> 37:58.706 align:middle
We realize that our own policies
affect performance in the rest

37:58.706 --> 38:01.966 align:middle
of the world, and
that performance

38:02.136 --> 38:04.916 align:middle
in other countries has
an influence on us,

38:05.256 --> 38:09.916 align:middle
so we spend a good deal of time
discussing global developments.

38:09.916 --> 38:13.286 align:middle
It is important for us to
keep our own house in order,

38:14.396 --> 38:18.526 align:middle
to put in place the
policy that's consistent

38:18.526 --> 38:22.036 align:middle
with the objectives that
Congress has given us,

38:22.036 --> 38:28.196 align:middle
and I think a strong U.S.
economy certainly is something

38:28.196 --> 38:30.786 align:middle
that is good for other
countries as well.

38:31.256 --> 38:34.876 align:middle
We have pledged to
communicate as clearly as we can

38:35.196 --> 38:39.886 align:middle
about monetary policy, and
I am trying to do that,

38:39.886 --> 38:41.446 align:middle
and will continue to do so.

38:42.676 --> 38:45.446 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

38:45.446 --> 38:47.576 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

38:47.666 --> 38:50.436 align:middle
We talked about the risk
of tightening too early.

38:50.746 --> 38:53.366 align:middle
What about the risk
of waiting too long,

38:53.366 --> 38:56.246 align:middle
especially since it can
take a while for Fed actions

38:56.246 --> 38:57.906 align:middle
to work their way
through the economy?

38:57.906 --> 38:58.666 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:58.836 --> 39:00.556 align:middle
Well, I agree with that.

39:00.736 --> 39:05.986 align:middle
Many, many studies over
decades and decades have showed

39:05.986 --> 39:10.796 align:middle
that there are lags in the way
monetary policy affects the

39:10.796 --> 39:14.886 align:middle
economy, and, therefore,
monetary policy does have

39:14.966 --> 39:16.286 align:middle
to be forward looking.

39:16.646 --> 39:20.406 align:middle
That's why we spend so much
time preparing forecasts

39:20.406 --> 39:24.506 align:middle
and discussing them, and we
want to put in place a policy

39:24.876 --> 39:29.306 align:middle
that will be appropriate for
where the economy is heading.

39:30.806 --> 39:35.836 align:middle
That's-that is, you
know, a reason that many

39:35.836 --> 39:39.996 align:middle
of my colleagues-most of my
colleagues-are anticipating

39:39.996 --> 39:42.886 align:middle
that it will be appropriate
to begin

39:42.886 --> 39:46.476 align:middle
to tighten policy
sometime this year.

39:46.716 --> 39:49.346 align:middle
In spite of the fact
that they are projecting

39:49.516 --> 39:53.416 align:middle
that inflation will be low,
they're looking forward

39:53.416 --> 39:58.056 align:middle
and they see that, by
the end of 2016 or 2017,

39:58.326 --> 40:02.786 align:middle
with the labor market
recovering, and assuming

40:02.786 --> 40:05.656 align:middle
that inflation expectations
remain stable

40:05.656 --> 40:12.616 align:middle
and transitory influences no
longer affecting inflation,

40:12.616 --> 40:16.306 align:middle
they see inflation heading back
to our 2 percent objective.

40:16.616 --> 40:22.656 align:middle
So just as we don't want to be
premature in tightening policy

40:22.656 --> 40:29.276 align:middle
and aborting a recovery that
we have worked long and hard

40:29.276 --> 40:33.146 align:middle
to proceed as far as it
has, we also don't want

40:33.146 --> 40:35.046 align:middle
to be behind the
curve in beginning

40:35.076 --> 40:36.976 align:middle
to tighten given those lags.

40:41.316 --> 40:42.986 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

40:43.836 --> 40:46.136 align:middle
Madame Chair, I'd like to
preface this by saying,

40:46.136 --> 40:48.296 align:middle
I do believe you stand
for accountability.

40:48.486 --> 40:52.356 align:middle
And-so, recently, a bunch of
us, we're in a room over there,

40:52.356 --> 40:54.016 align:middle
there was a police
guard outside,

40:54.016 --> 40:56.306 align:middle
and your staff had
taken our cell phones,

40:56.306 --> 40:59.036 align:middle
and they controlled the
Internet in the room-all this

40:59.036 --> 41:01.686 align:middle
to guard the security
of the FOMC statement.

41:02.336 --> 41:04.706 align:middle
If one of us had leaked
it, we would lose our jobs.

41:04.816 --> 41:08.576 align:middle
Surely there would be a
prosecution, and my friends here

41:08.576 --> 41:10.726 align:middle
in the press would
certainly have a banner day

41:10.726 --> 41:11.456 align:middle
with that story.

41:11.456 --> 41:14.066 align:middle
There was a leak in the FOMC.

41:14.066 --> 41:16.226 align:middle
We don't know what happened.

41:16.506 --> 41:19.696 align:middle
I've asked, I can't
get an answer.

41:20.326 --> 41:22.366 align:middle
And now Congress is asking.

41:22.476 --> 41:23.676 align:middle
Both parties want to know.

41:23.826 --> 41:27.096 align:middle
I'm not going to ask about the
IG's probe-I understand that's

41:27.096 --> 41:31.526 align:middle
an active case now,
suddenly, after two years

41:31.526 --> 41:32.566 align:middle
of just sitting there.

41:32.666 --> 41:35.246 align:middle
But I would like to ask what
you've found at the Board.

41:35.526 --> 41:38.006 align:middle
You weren't Chairman then, you
were Vice Chair, I believe.

41:39.226 --> 41:41.826 align:middle
So what answers do you
have, and are you going

41:41.826 --> 41:42.906 align:middle
to respond to Congress?

41:43.676 --> 41:44.256 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

41:44.706 --> 41:48.296 align:middle
So, let me say that the
Committee and I, personally,

41:48.906 --> 41:52.046 align:middle
take very seriously
our responsibility

41:52.096 --> 41:55.156 align:middle
to safeguard confidential
information.

41:56.086 --> 42:01.186 align:middle
We have a set of policies and
procedures that are in place

42:01.676 --> 42:04.236 align:middle
that we're to follow
if we believe

42:04.336 --> 42:10.436 align:middle
that there have been leaks
of confidential information.

42:10.866 --> 42:15.766 align:middle
And this is something that
doesn't occur very often,

42:16.166 --> 42:21.026 align:middle
but if it does occur, we
follow those procedures.

42:21.706 --> 42:26.776 align:middle
It has been reported that our
Inspector General is engaged

42:26.776 --> 42:32.636 align:middle
in a review at this time of
this matter, and in light

42:32.636 --> 42:36.636 align:middle
of that ongoing review, I'm
not going to get into details,

42:36.676 --> 42:39.946 align:middle
but let me just say that
we welcome that review

42:39.946 --> 42:44.046 align:middle
and are looking forward
to its conclusions.

42:44.456 --> 42:46.156 align:middle
With respect to Congress,

42:46.516 --> 42:52.346 align:middle
congressional inquiries-we
have arranged to brief members

42:52.346 --> 42:54.366 align:middle
of Congress who've
asked about this,

42:54.366 --> 42:57.886 align:middle
and we'll certainly
cooperate in trying

42:57.886 --> 43:00.516 align:middle
to provide them the
information that they seek.

43:01.126 --> 43:02.026 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

43:09.356 --> 43:12.366 align:middle
Madame Chair, thanks
for taking my question.

43:14.026 --> 43:18.146 align:middle
The banking sector has clearly
improved since the crisis

43:18.146 --> 43:20.786 align:middle
in terms of capital retention,

43:21.496 --> 43:24.896 align:middle
but there's also
seemingly a number

43:24.896 --> 43:30.126 align:middle
of scandals involving forex
manipulation, of course LIBOR.

43:30.406 --> 43:32.056 align:middle
Do you think that the culture

43:32.426 --> 43:34.806 align:middle
at the banks is where
it ought to be?

43:35.396 --> 43:38.336 align:middle
And, if not, what is
the Fed going to do

43:38.456 --> 43:40.036 align:middle
to improve it, and when?

43:40.896 --> 43:41.516 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

43:41.886 --> 43:45.406 align:middle
Well, it's certainly
been-I mean,

43:45.406 --> 43:48.066 align:middle
it's certainly been
very disappointing

43:48.126 --> 43:54.576 align:middle
to see what have been some
really brazen violations

43:54.576 --> 43:59.876 align:middle
of the law, and we
absolutely expect the banks

43:59.876 --> 44:03.386 align:middle
that we supervise to comply
with the law and to have

44:03.386 --> 44:09.326 align:middle
in place controls that ensure
compliance in organizations.

44:09.896 --> 44:12.536 align:middle
And while changing the culture

44:12.536 --> 44:16.386 align:middle
of organizations is not
something that we can achieve

44:16.696 --> 44:22.046 align:middle
through supervision, we will
make sure that the banks

44:22.046 --> 44:25.626 align:middle
that we supervise have
appropriate compliance regimes

44:25.906 --> 44:26.916 align:middle
in place.

44:27.016 --> 44:30.436 align:middle
And to the extent

44:30.436 --> 44:36.716 align:middle
that compensation schemes might
be incenting such behavior

44:36.976 --> 44:42.856 align:middle
that inappropriately rewards
risk-taking, that's something

44:42.856 --> 44:47.406 align:middle
that was-we will, you know, look
for in our supervision as well.

44:47.716 --> 44:48.426 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

44:48.426 --> 44:49.276 align:middle
I have a follow-up.

44:49.356 --> 44:57.006 align:middle
When you introduced-you
introduced a compensation rule

44:57.006 --> 44:58.566 align:middle
in 2011.

44:58.986 --> 45:00.536 align:middle
When do you think we
might see some movement

45:00.666 --> 45:01.376 align:middle
on that-on that rule?

45:01.376 --> 45:01.696 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

45:01.846 --> 45:06.696 align:middle
Well, the agencies-the agencies
are working jointly to bring

45:06.696 --> 45:11.076 align:middle
out a rule on this, but we
do have supervisory policies

45:11.376 --> 45:15.916 align:middle
in place concerning the
structure of incentive pay

45:15.916 --> 45:19.996 align:middle
and compensation, and
our supervision covers

45:19.996 --> 45:21.856 align:middle
that topic now.

45:22.086 --> 45:26.426 align:middle
And we have seen, I think,
meaningful changes already

45:26.426 --> 45:30.816 align:middle
in the structure of compensation
and banking organizations

45:31.126 --> 45:35.056 align:middle
to diminish ways in which
it might incent risk-taking.

45:36.256 --> 45:40.846 align:middle
PEDRO DA COSTA.

45:40.996 --> 45:42.736 align:middle
Pedro da Costa with
Dow Jones Newswires.

45:42.736 --> 45:44.196 align:middle
I guess I have two follow-ups,

45:44.196 --> 45:46.186 align:middle
one with regard to
Craig's question.

45:47.976 --> 45:50.236 align:middle
So, before the IG's
investigation,

45:50.236 --> 45:54.256 align:middle
according to Republican
Congressman Hensarling's letter

45:54.256 --> 45:57.336 align:middle
to your office, he says
that, "It is my understanding

45:57.336 --> 45:59.616 align:middle
that although the Federal
Reserve's General Counsel was

45:59.616 --> 46:01.306 align:middle
initially involved in
this investigation,

46:01.826 --> 46:03.476 align:middle
the inquiry was dropped
at the request

46:03.476 --> 46:05.186 align:middle
of several members of the FOMC."

46:05.186 --> 46:06.786 align:middle
Now, that predates the IG.

46:07.246 --> 46:09.286 align:middle
I want to know if you could
tell us who are these members

46:09.286 --> 46:12.096 align:middle
of the FOMC who struck
down this investigation?

46:12.856 --> 46:16.336 align:middle
And doesn't not revealing
these facts kind of go directly

46:16.336 --> 46:18.276 align:middle
against the sort of
transparency and accountability

46:18.276 --> 46:19.736 align:middle
that you're trying to
bring to the central bank?

46:19.736 --> 46:21.056 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:21.056 --> 46:22.566 align:middle
That is an allegation

46:22.566 --> 46:26.156 align:middle
that I don't believe
has any basis in fact.

46:26.426 --> 46:30.316 align:middle
I'm not going to go into the
details, but I don't know

46:30.316 --> 46:34.016 align:middle
where that piece of information
could possibly have come from.

46:34.456 --> 46:36.346 align:middle
PEDRO DA COSTA.

46:36.666 --> 46:38.966 align:middle
If I could follow
up on his question.

46:39.126 --> 46:43.236 align:middle
I think when you get asked
about financial crimes

46:43.236 --> 46:46.646 align:middle
and the public hears you
talk about compliance,

46:47.546 --> 46:50.406 align:middle
you get a sense that there's
not enough enforcement involved

46:50.406 --> 46:54.476 align:middle
in these actions, and that it's
merely a case of kind of trying

46:54.476 --> 46:57.326 align:middle
to achieve settlements
after the fact.

46:57.326 --> 47:00.726 align:middle
Is there a sense in the
regulatory community

47:00.796 --> 47:04.736 align:middle
that financial crimes need to be
punished sort of more forcefully

47:04.736 --> 47:07.736 align:middle
in order for them to be-for
there to be an actual deterrent

47:07.736 --> 47:09.096 align:middle
against unethical behavior?

47:09.956 --> 47:10.476 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

47:11.096 --> 47:13.856 align:middle
So, the-you're talking about
within banking organizations?

47:14.276 --> 47:23.126 align:middle
So, the focus of regulators-the
banking regulators-is safety

47:23.126 --> 47:31.156 align:middle
and soundness, and what we
want to see is changes made

47:31.156 --> 47:35.826 align:middle
as rapidly as possible that
will eliminate practices

47:35.826 --> 47:39.166 align:middle
that are unsafe and unsound.

47:39.466 --> 47:43.706 align:middle
We can't-only the Justice
Department can bring criminal

47:43.706 --> 47:49.326 align:middle
action, and they have taken
up cases where they think

47:49.326 --> 47:50.566 align:middle
that that's appropriate.

47:51.986 --> 47:57.006 align:middle
In some situations, when we are
able to identify individuals

47:57.416 --> 48:00.756 align:middle
who were responsible
for misdeeds,

48:01.466 --> 48:06.646 align:middle
we can put in place prohibitions
that bar them from participating

48:06.646 --> 48:11.016 align:middle
in banking, and we have done
so and will continue to do so.

48:11.546 --> 48:17.146 align:middle
STEVE BECKNER.

48:17.476 --> 48:19.556 align:middle
Steve Beckner of MNI.

48:19.646 --> 48:21.486 align:middle
Good afternoon, Madame Chair.

48:21.486 --> 48:24.686 align:middle
The FOMC said last September
that it will wait 'til

48:24.686 --> 48:27.586 align:middle
after the first rate
hike to stop

48:27.836 --> 48:32.726 align:middle
or to discontinue reinvesting
proceeds of its MBS holdings

48:32.726 --> 48:36.196 align:middle
and stop rolling over
maturing Treasuries.

48:36.676 --> 48:40.006 align:middle
What is the FOMC's current
thinking about how long

48:40.006 --> 48:42.076 align:middle
after liftoff you should wait

48:42.076 --> 48:44.456 align:middle
to stop reinvestments
and rollovers?

48:44.746 --> 48:46.776 align:middle
And, given the very
large amounts

48:46.776 --> 48:51.836 align:middle
of Treasuries maturing next
year, would it make sense

48:51.836 --> 48:56.566 align:middle
for the FOMC to vary the pace
of runoff that it allows?

48:57.836 --> 48:58.636 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:59.916 --> 49:04.246 align:middle
So, we issued in September a
set of normalization principles,

49:04.756 --> 49:09.726 align:middle
and, as you noted, the
Committee indicated

49:09.806 --> 49:15.056 align:middle
that we will eventually
cease reinvestments

49:15.056 --> 49:18.846 align:middle
or diminish the pace of
reinvestments as a way

49:18.846 --> 49:24.166 align:middle
of gradually reducing the size
of our portfolio over time.

49:24.806 --> 49:29.376 align:middle
We said we would do that
when economic conditions were

49:29.376 --> 49:32.576 align:middle
appropriate after we
begin raising rates,

49:32.626 --> 49:36.646 align:middle
because we want changes
in the-our target range

49:36.646 --> 49:40.156 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate to be the main tool

49:40.156 --> 49:44.006 align:middle
by which we shift the
stance of monetary policy.

49:44.436 --> 49:47.946 align:middle
We've not made any
decision at this point

49:48.256 --> 49:53.936 align:middle
about how long it will be,
once we begin to raise rates,

49:53.936 --> 49:59.526 align:middle
before we reduce or
cease reinvestment.

49:59.866 --> 50:04.486 align:middle
We will see how things go, and
the Committee will revisit that

50:04.766 --> 50:07.116 align:middle
and make a decision
at a later time.

50:07.466 --> 50:12.316 align:middle
You also indicated that we
have a substantial quantity

50:12.316 --> 50:15.446 align:middle
of Treasuries that will
roll off our balance sheet

50:15.796 --> 50:17.356 align:middle
over the next several years.

50:17.356 --> 50:22.896 align:middle
That's true, and that will-I
think, over the next two years,

50:22.896 --> 50:26.406 align:middle
almost $800 billion
will mature-and

50:27.046 --> 50:30.796 align:middle
that they will be
short-term, obviously,

50:32.146 --> 50:35.476 align:middle
Treasuries at that
point, and that's a way

50:35.476 --> 50:38.416 align:middle
in which we anticipate
diminishing the size

50:38.416 --> 50:39.506 align:middle
of our portfolio.

50:40.096 --> 50:40.716 align:middle
STEVE BECKNER.

50:40.876 --> 50:44.596 align:middle
Can I follow up, please?

50:47.386 --> 50:49.506 align:middle
Some people have suggested
that you might need

50:49.506 --> 50:54.346 align:middle
to manage those runoffs
a little more granularly,

50:54.346 --> 51:00.606 align:middle
if that's a word, perhaps pursue
a different track for Treasuries

51:00.606 --> 51:04.216 align:middle
versus MBS or, you
know, at given quarters

51:04.216 --> 51:06.736 align:middle
when you have very
large amounts maturing,

51:06.736 --> 51:09.326 align:middle
and there might be a spike
in long-term interest rates,

51:09.616 --> 51:12.696 align:middle
that maybe you would
vary the rate of runoff.

51:12.696 --> 51:15.656 align:middle
Is that-any consideration
given to that?

51:15.886 --> 51:16.176 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

51:16.836 --> 51:20.986 align:middle
That's something for
which we've made no plans,

51:20.986 --> 51:26.616 align:middle
and I don't really have
anything for you on that.

51:27.036 --> 51:28.226 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

51:29.556 --> 51:35.636 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

51:37.756 --> 51:41.216 align:middle
We hear that productivity takes
a long time before you can

51:41.216 --> 51:44.306 align:middle
understand it, but it's
been very low in this cycle.

51:44.756 --> 51:46.676 align:middle
What does that mean
for Fed policy?

51:47.496 --> 51:47.876 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

51:47.876 --> 51:51.806 align:middle
Well, I agree, it has
been very low-it's been

51:51.806 --> 51:53.776 align:middle
disappointingly low.

51:54.666 --> 52:01.136 align:middle
A positive aspect of what is
fundamentally a disappointment

52:01.596 --> 52:05.436 align:middle
is that the labor market
has improved more rapidly

52:05.806 --> 52:08.756 align:middle
than might have been
expected given the pace

52:08.756 --> 52:09.986 align:middle
of economic growth.

52:11.006 --> 52:17.016 align:middle
So the unemployment rate
has come down more rapidly

52:17.016 --> 52:18.506 align:middle
than I would have expected,

52:18.806 --> 52:21.436 align:middle
and the labor market has
improved more rapidly

52:21.696 --> 52:23.326 align:middle
than I would have expected.

52:25.106 --> 52:31.076 align:middle
We have written down our
estimates of potential output.

52:31.076 --> 52:40.856 align:middle
In the long run, it is
a disappointing factor

52:41.086 --> 52:43.036 align:middle
about the ultimate prospects

52:43.036 --> 52:45.876 align:middle
for the U.S. economy
if it continues.

52:46.286 --> 52:49.496 align:middle
I would expect it to pick
up, and as you can see

52:49.566 --> 52:52.406 align:middle
from the longer-run
growth projections,

52:52.406 --> 52:57.356 align:middle
most FOMC participants
believe it will pick

52:57.356 --> 52:59.846 align:middle
up above current levels.

53:00.306 --> 53:04.536 align:middle
But it means it's something
that would, if it persists,

53:04.686 --> 53:06.406 align:middle
retard living standards

53:06.696 --> 53:10.946 align:middle
and would likely retard real
wage growth and improvement

53:12.016 --> 53:14.976 align:middle
in living standards for
ordinary households.

53:15.486 --> 53:17.256 align:middle
PETER COOK.

53:18.866 --> 53:21.776 align:middle
Peter Cook of Bloomberg
Television.

53:22.406 --> 53:24.776 align:middle
Madame Chair, one clarification,
if I could, logistically,

53:24.776 --> 53:27.496 align:middle
and then a question about
Congress, a follow-up.

53:27.576 --> 53:28.886 align:middle
First of all, you've
been asked this before,

53:28.886 --> 53:30.086 align:middle
I just want to see
if we can clarify.

53:30.586 --> 53:32.616 align:middle
When you decide to start
raising interest rates,

53:32.746 --> 53:34.856 align:middle
could that decision
happen at a meeting

53:34.896 --> 53:37.146 align:middle
in which you-it's not
followed by a press conference?

53:37.706 --> 53:39.576 align:middle
And, on my congressional
question,

53:40.376 --> 53:41.696 align:middle
I would like to get
your reaction

53:41.696 --> 53:43.336 align:middle
to your-the treatment
you received

53:43.336 --> 53:44.696 align:middle
up on Capitol Hill
the other day.

53:44.696 --> 53:46.506 align:middle
It didn't look like a
very pleasant experience,

53:46.506 --> 53:48.446 align:middle
certainly in front of the
House, at least at moments.

53:49.096 --> 53:51.866 align:middle
I wondered if you have
concerns about that.

53:51.866 --> 53:54.806 align:middle
Has the relationship between the
Fed and Congress deteriorated

53:54.806 --> 53:56.616 align:middle
to a point that it
causes you concern?

53:57.106 --> 53:58.936 align:middle
What, if anything, can
you do about it as well?

53:58.986 --> 53:59.436 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

53:59.806 --> 54:02.076 align:middle
So let me start with
the press conferences.

54:02.076 --> 54:05.006 align:middle
I said this previously,
and let me reiterate it,

54:05.526 --> 54:09.176 align:middle
that every meeting that the
Federal Open Market Committee

54:09.546 --> 54:13.966 align:middle
has is a live meeting at which
we could make a decision.

54:15.266 --> 54:18.556 align:middle
Clearly, if we decided
for the first time

54:18.556 --> 54:20.406 align:middle
to raise the federal funds rate,

54:20.866 --> 54:23.656 align:middle
it is something-I think
it would be appropriate

54:23.756 --> 54:28.036 align:middle
to answer questions and
explain in more detail.

54:28.626 --> 54:33.896 align:middle
We have long had the capacity
to call a press conference

54:33.896 --> 54:36.286 align:middle
after a meeting that
we would hold

54:36.366 --> 54:42.026 align:middle
by teleconference-by conference
call-and that's a capacity

54:42.026 --> 54:44.736 align:middle
that was used on a
number of occasions

54:44.736 --> 54:48.786 align:middle
by my predecessor during
the financial crisis.

54:48.786 --> 54:53.426 align:middle
It is something that
remains a capacity we have

54:53.776 --> 54:57.106 align:middle
and would expect to use
if it were necessary.

54:57.906 --> 55:02.466 align:middle
On the second part of your
question, with respect

55:02.536 --> 55:07.586 align:middle
to testimony, you know,
it's very important

55:07.586 --> 55:10.696 align:middle
for the Federal Reserve to
be accountable to Congress.

55:11.136 --> 55:14.836 align:middle
We have a wide range
of responsibilities,

55:15.156 --> 55:22.406 align:middle
and it's entirely appropriate
for me to testify and be quizzed

55:22.406 --> 55:26.116 align:middle
on a range of topics
by members of Congress.

55:26.526 --> 55:31.856 align:middle
I-you know, I think I need to
be ready to answer questions

55:31.856 --> 55:35.206 align:middle
on any aspect of
Federal Reserve behavior,

55:35.206 --> 55:37.576 align:middle
and that's an important
principle.

